quiz:
  - q: 'A \(\$100\) million fund holds \(\$80\) million of longs and \(\$50\) million of shorts. What are its gross and net exposure?'
    opts: ['Gross \(130\%\), net \(30\%\) long', 'Gross \(30\%\), net \(130\%\) long', 'Gross \(80\%\), net \(50\%\) long']
    right: 0
    why: 'Gross adds both sides: \((80 + 50) \div 100 = 130\%\). Net subtracts: \((80 - 50) \div 100 = 30\%\) net long, so the fund catches roughly \(30\%\) of the market''s moves.'
  - q: 'A fund''s longs are mostly jumpy tech stocks and its shorts are mostly steady consumer stocks. It''s exactly \(0\%\) net. Is it market-neutral?'
    opts: ['Yes: zero net means zero market risk', 'No: its beta-adjusted net is positive, so it still rises and falls with the market', 'No: it''s actually net short']
    right: 1
    why: 'Equal money long and short isn''t the same as equal risk. High-beta longs move more than low-beta shorts, so the book still leans long the market. That''s why funds track beta-adjusted net, not just dollar net.'
  - q: 'Why would a fund short a momentum factor ETF such as MTUM alongside its stock picks?'
    opts: ['Because it expects the whole market to fall', 'To earn the short rebate on the cash', 'To cancel an accidental bet that recent winners keep winning, leaving only its stock-specific view']
    right: 2
    why: 'If most of the longs are recent winners, the book is quietly betting on the momentum factor. A momentum crash would hit it however good the picks are. Shorting the factor removes that bet.'

